 and when we first sat down  to film this one sean  being someone who like really i told him  on the day when we were filming it like  i wanted to work with him because sean  can make anything look pretty uh he's  also a photographer  um i worked with him at ign and he shot  unboxings that he made look beautiful i  was like i know that  i can you can make anything look pretty  as the director it is my job to to like  be like okay here's how i want this  setup and here's why kind of thing  um and he for this scene i think was  originally like why don't we like shoot  you over the shoulder looking out to the  city  and i was like no it's going to be the  exact opposite i'm going to sit right  here on this balcony  in between all of this um  make sure that i'm really hunched over  that my shoulders are up like very very  very intentional with the posture on  this one  um and and yeah just make sure that i'm  framed within these frames almost like a  little bit of  a prison and it was really specific with  the lunchtime shot  um again this isn't based on my life but  it is  experiences that i'm pulling from which  is generally how most writing works  um is that the lunchtime is a thing that  i feel like  in the real world outside of isolation  was something that i would look forward  to  in this instance during the pandemic  lunch is  just being in an apartment and eating in  silence  that's really was my experience and that  was one thing that you know  at this point if this is lunchtime we've  had the the monotony of working in the  morning and  eating lunch by yourself it's just um  yeah something that i feel like  a lot of days lunchtime was a low point  for me um frankly i would say i worked  through most lunch breaks  uh but yeah this shot is intended to  feel like someone  caged um not being  able to access the world that is behind  them and kind of looming over them  especially as someone  uh with a series of chronic illnesses um  it's a very very very specific 